{
  "id": "code-subtree-of-another-tree",
  "title": "Subtree of Another Tree",
  "difficulty": "easy",
  "topics": ["tree", "depth-first search", "string matching"],
  "asked": "common",
  "statement": "You're given the roots of two binary trees, `root` and `sub_root`. Return `True` if some node of `root`, together with everything below it, forms a tree identical to `sub_root` in shape and values. Otherwise return `False`.\n\nA tree counts as a subtree of itself. Matching only the top part of a subtree isn't enough: every descendant has to match too. Each node is a `TreeNode` with `val`, `left` and `right`; the class is already defined for you. In the examples, a tree is written in level order with `null` for a missing child.\n\nConstraints: `root` has 1 to 2000 nodes and `sub_root` has 1 to 1000 nodes; -10^4 <= val <= 10^4.",
  "functionName": "is_subtree",
  "signature": "def is_subtree(root: Optional[TreeNode], sub_root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> bool:",
  "starterCode": "def is_subtree(root: Optional[TreeNode], sub_root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Return True if sub_root appears as a complete subtree of root.\"\"\"\n    pass\n",
  "examples": [
    {"input": "root = [5, 3, 8, 1, 4], sub_root = [3, 1, 4]", "output": "true", "explanation": "The node 3 in root has exactly the children 1 and 4, and nothing below them."},
    {"input": "root = [5, 3, 8, 1, 4, null, null, null, null, 2], sub_root = [3, 1, 4]", "output": "false", "explanation": "Node 3 in root also has a 2 under its child 4, so its subtree is bigger than sub_root."},
    {"input": "root = [1, 4, 4, 1, null, 2, 3], sub_root = [4, 2, 3]", "output": "true", "explanation": "The first 4 doesn't match, but the second one does; keep searching after a failed match."}
  ],
  "visibleTestCount": 3,
  "tests": [
