  "compare": "exact",
  "adapters": {"root": "tree", "sub_root": "tree"},
  "referenceSolution": "def is_subtree(root: Optional[TreeNode], sub_root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> bool:\n    def encode(node):\n        out, stack = [], [node]\n        while stack:\n            current = stack.pop()\n            if current is None:\n                out.append(\",#\")\n            else:\n                out.append(f\",{current.val}\")\n                stack.append(current.right)\n                stack.append(current.left)\n        return \"\".join(out)\n\n    return encode(sub_root) in encode(root)\n",
  "targetComplexity": {"time": "O(m + n)", "space": "O(m + n)"},
  "hints": [
    "Start with a smaller question: given two nodes, how would you check that the trees under them are identical?",
    "Now try that check from every node of root. Where should the search go after a node fails to match?",
    "Matching a node's value isn't enough; the whole shape below it must match, including missing children. Look at the second example.",
    "If you turn each tree into a string to compare, check `root = [12]` and `sub_root = [2]`: how do you stop '2' from matching the end of '12'?",
    "Brute force runs a same-tree check from every node, O(m * n). Faster: serialize both trees in preorder with a marker for every missing child and a delimiter before every value, then check whether one string contains the other."
  ],
  "followUps": [
    "What is the worst case of the node-by-node approach, and what kind of tree triggers it?",
    "If you serialize the trees, why do you need markers for missing children and a delimiter in front of each value?",
    "Is Python's `in` on strings guaranteed to be linear, and what would you use if you needed that guarantee?",
    "How would you solve this by hashing every subtree, and what risk do hash collisions add?"
  ],
  "idealAnswerPoints": [
    "Builds on a same-tree helper tried from every node, or serializes both trees with null markers",
    "Requires the whole subtree to match, not just the top nodes",
    "Puts a delimiter before each value so 2 can't match inside 12",
    "Names O(m * n) for the brute force and O(m + n) for serialization with linear string matching"
  ],
  "weakAnswerSigns": [
    "Stops searching after the first node whose value matches",
    "Serializes without null markers or delimiters and gets false positives",
    "Accepts a match on the top of a subtree that has extra nodes below"
  ],
  "rubric": {
    "correctness": "Right answer when values repeat, when a candidate subtree has extra nodes below, and for multi-digit and negative values.",
    "efficiency": "O(m * n) brute force is acceptable; O(m + n) with serialization and substring search is the strong answer.",
    "code_quality": "A reusable same-tree helper or one serializer used for both trees, with no per-value special cases.",
    "communication": "Explains the difference between matching a node and matching a whole subtree, and the false-positive risk of naive string matching."
  }
